-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 0085 — A ROADSTEAD LIES ON THE CHANNEL
--        A port the carve reaches is reached from the channel itself, not from the centre of a
--        cell the land data calls land — and the river ports get their line and their ring.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--
-- GENERATED by scripts/build-sea-migration.mjs — do not hand-edit. Change the sea (scripts/
-- sea-grid.mjs) or the ports and cut a NEW migration; an applied one is history.
--
-- ── THE OWNER, VERBATIM (docs/OWNER_REQUESTS.md, row 78, 2026-09-13) ─────────────────────────────
--   "in map, the circle should point out to ocean, but london for example the circle is in
--    land. What is the point of the circle then?"
--
-- ── WHAT SAYS THE OPPOSITE, NAMED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   * 0076/0079 seeded every off-quay roadstead as the CENTRE of the nearest sailable cell
--     (scripts/build-sea-migration.mjs, the navCellLat/navCellLon arm; voyage.water_roadstead
--     0076:207-208). London's nearest sailable cell is (51.375, -0.125): 8.11 nm from the quay,
--     8 nm SOUTH of the Thames, inside the United Kingdom polygon of data/world-110m.json and
--     36 nm from its nearest coastline. That cell is water for one reason only — the authored
--     `thames-scheldt` CHANNEL carved it (scripts/sea-grid.mjs, point [51.5, -0.1]); the
--     scan-filled land data (preCarveGrid) says land. The chart drew the ring exactly where it
--     was served (src/lib/geo/projection.ts, src/chart/roadsteads.ts), so this is a server
--     derivation defect and not a drawing defect.
--   * 0079's self-assert (d), "every roadstead stands on sailable water", asked the CARVED raster
--     — which is why it passed on a point the raster's own source polygon calls land.
--   * A port whose OWN cell the carve opened (Seville, Antwerp, Nantes, Bordeaux and twelve
--     more) was seeded at snap 0 as its own roadstead, so no dotted line and no ring was ever
--     drawn for a river port at all.
--   * scripts/db/proof-courses.mjs carried a SECOND copy of the roadstead rule (its own
--     roadsteadOf, snapping the raster itself). Folded in the same change: the proofs now read
--     public.sea_reaches, the one served answer, and re-derive nothing.
--
-- ── THE RULE (one function each side: roadsteadOf in the generator, voyage.water_roadstead here) ─
--   1. The nearest sailable cell is found as 0076 found it: snapToNav / the 12-ring loop below,
--      the minimum-distance cell.
--   2. If that cell was water BEFORE any carve, the roadstead is what 0076 said — the quay itself
--      at 0 nm when the quay's own cell is water, otherwise the cell centre.
--   3. If that cell is CARVED — sea in the raster, land in the data — the roadstead is THE POINT
--      ON THE CARVING CHANNEL'S POLYLINE NEAREST THE QUAY, rounded to 3 dp, and snap_nm is
--      measured to THAT point, so gc(quay, roadstead) = snap_nm still holds on every row and the
--      helper line the chart draws is still the distance the table measured. The whole polyline
--      is searched, not the part inside the cell: over all 25 such places the two answers
--      differ by under 0.2 nm except Riga (4.53 vs 4.69) and Tallinn (0.77 vs 1.40), and the
--      unconstrained foot is the simpler rule to state in two languages and prove equal.
--   The channel polylines have ONE source, scripts/sea-grid.mjs CHANNELS. They cross the wire
--   into voyage.channels below — with the cells of dry land each one opened, which SQL cannot
--   re-derive because it does not hold the land data — so the SQL twin answers the same point.
--   sea-grid.mjs still declares no snap rule (tests/duplication.spec.ts): it says what the carve
--   DID, never where a roadstead is.
--
-- ── WHAT THIS DOES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   1. voyage.channels — the 27 authored channels: id, name, the polyline, the 202 cells of
--      dry land the carve opened (as sea_raster cell indices), and opens_land, CHECKed equal to
--      that list's length. Server-only, replaced whole on every run like sea_reaches.
--   2. voyage.channel_foot(channel, lat, lon) — the point on one channel nearest a point, at
--      3 dp, with its distance. The generator's channelFoot, operation for operation.
--   3. voyage.water_roadstead is DROPPED and RE-CREATED with a fourth column, channel: the
--      12-ring search is 0076's unchanged; the winning cell is then looked up in voyage.channels
--      and, if the carve opened it, the answer is channel_foot on that channel. water_snap_nm
--      (one line over it) is untouched and still answers the distance.
--   4. public.sea_reaches is replaced WHOLE — snap_nm, the roadstead columns, and `reaches`
--      re-measured roadstead to roadstead (the 0052:92-94 delete-and-insert, exactly), because
--      24 roadsteads move and every passage to or from them is measured from the new point.
--
-- ── WHAT IT SUPERSEDES, AND WHY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   voyage.water_roadstead(numeric, numeric), created at 0076:175. This file SUPERSEDES it: the
--   return shape gains `channel text` (null unless rule 3 applied), which `create or replace`
--   cannot do, so it is dropped and re-created and its revoke re-issued (docs/NO_SPAGHETTI.md §3,
--   "re-issue the grants, because a dropped function takes its ACL with it"). Its one caller,
--   voyage.water_snap_nm(numeric, numeric) (0076:227), selects `nm` by name and is untouched.
--   The supersede is a NO-OP for every place whose nearest sailable cell the land data also calls
--   water — asserted below by requiring the seeded point to equal the function's answer on all
--   238 rows, of which 213 are exactly what 0079 seeded.
--   Nothing else is re-cut: cmd.do_sail, voyage.assert_paths_water and world.snapshot read
--   sea_reaches and serve the new point without a change of body.
--
-- ── AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   * It does not move the roadstead of any place whose nearest sailable cell is water in the
--     land data too — 213 of 238, Amsterdam, Lisbon, Cadiz and Plymouth among them.
--   * It does not walk a river port's roadstead SEAWARD to the coastline polygon. Measured and
--     not chosen: London's would land 52 nm out at (51.539, 1.263), Hamburg's 53 nm out, and the
--     whole Guadalquivir and Yangon channels lie inside their polygons so Seville, Sanlucar and
--     Thanlyin would have no such point at all. The course begins at the roadstead and sails the
--     carved river (0076); moving the roadstead to the river mouth would move the start of every
--     such passage, which is a movement-model change and not this row.
--   * It does not touch the drawn coastline. A further ~10 roadsteads sit in polygon water but
--     inside the DRAWN coast, because src/chart/coastlineBuild.ts decimates the coastline
--     (COASTLINE_TOLERANCE_DEG). That is a chart defect and is left for its own change; moving a
--     correct roadstead to suit a simplified drawing would be the wrong authority moving.
--   * It opens and closes no CHANNEL, moves no price knob, re-cuts no mover, touches no player row.
--   * IT DOES NOT MOVE THE WATER. The grid built by this run is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the one
--     public.sea_raster already holds (545,966 water cells, 259200 packed bytes, +0 opened,
--     -0 closed), so the raster row is not rewritten. Only what the table SAYS about that
--     water changes.
--
-- ── EVIDENCE, MEASURED BY THIS RUN AGAINST THE APPLIED CHAIN ───────────────────────────────────
--   THE LAND DATA, asked of every off-quay roadstead the chain serves today (RED): 8 stand inside
